# SPACE FACTORY — line endings and binary handling
#
# Why this file exists: the repo is worked by agents on different platforms
# (Cursor, a cloud lore agent on Linux, Claude Code on Windows) with
# core.autocrlf=true locally and no attributes, which produced three different
# states across 374 text files: most at LF, 48 checked out CRLF, and 26 of the
# original hand-authored scripts with CRLF committed *into the index*. Mixed
# index endings mean a whole-file diff the moment any tool rewrites one.
#
# Fix: LF everywhere, in the index and in the working tree. Unity itself writes
# LF for .cs, .meta, .unity, .prefab and .asset on every platform, so this
# matches the engine rather than fighting it, and Visual Studio / Rider handle
# LF on Windows fine.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Source
*.cs        text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.shader    text eol=lf
*.cginc     text eol=lf
*.hlsl      text eol=lf
*.compute   text eol=lf

# Unity YAML + project files
*.meta      text eol=lf
*.unity     text eol=lf
*.prefab    text eol=lf
*.asset     text eol=lf
*.mat       text eol=lf
*.anim      text eol=lf
*.controller text eol=lf
*.physicMaterial text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# Scene/prefab merges are not safe to line-merge by hand. If three-way merges
# on Unity YAML ever become a problem, configure Unity's SmartMerge and switch
# the four types above to `merge=unityyamlmerge`.

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF so they run under cmd/PowerShell hosts that
# care. (lore/sync-assets-sheet.ps1 and friends.)
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# Binaries — never touch, never diff as text
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.tga       binary
*.psd       binary
*.tif       binary
*.exr       binary
*.hdr       binary
*.fbx       binary
*.obj       binary
*.blend     binary
*.wav       binary
*.mp3       binary
*.ogg       binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.dll       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary
*.pdb       binary
*.unitypackage binary
*.zip       binary

# Kenney's OBJ/MTL are plain text but git's `text=auto` heuristic guesses them
# binary and then stores the working-tree bytes verbatim — which reintroduced
# CRLF into an index that was already LF, rewriting every mesh file for nothing.
# Declaring them text explicitly overrides the guess and leaves the index alone.
*.obj       text eol=lf
*.mtl       text eol=lf

# This one vendored license carries a stray lone CR (an old Mac line ending)
# mid-file, which makes eol normalization non-idempotent: it re-checks-out
# dirty no matter how many times it is restored. Pin it byte-exact and stop
# converting a file we should not be rewriting anyway.
Assets/Art/ThirdParty/Kenney_SpaceKit/License.txt -text
